Prevalence and the normalization mechanism, configuration ariel, pipeline norm_xgb

1. Precision at realistic base rates. The grid is balanced by construction, so every
   accuracy in the paper assumes a 50 % prevalence. Recall and specificity are properties
   of the classifier and do not change with prevalence; precision does.

   threshold  recall  specif.    π=0.5    π=0.2    π=0.1   π=0.05   π=0.01
       0.500   97.6%    94.5%    94.7%    81.6%    66.3%    48.3%    15.2%
       0.215   99.1%    90.6%    91.3%    72.5%    54.0%    35.7%     9.6%
       0.900   89.6%    98.3%    98.2%    93.1%    85.7%    73.9%    35.2%
       0.990   73.1%    99.6%    99.5%    98.0%    95.6%    91.1%    66.2%

   at π = 0.10, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.141 and retains 99.4 % recall
   at π = 0.05, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.554 and retains 97.1 % recall
   at π = 0.01, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.970 and retains 82.8 % recall

2. Why per-spectrum normalization helps: the absolute-depth nuisance direction.
   Raw vs normalized features trained and tested on radius-restricted populations,
   training-set size held fixed at 4,000 planets per band.

    radius (R_E)      raw   normalized      gap   depth span
            1-26   81.05%       95.23%  +14.18      4.49 dex
             1-6   75.57%       94.81%  +19.23      4.24 dex
            6-12   84.44%       95.02%  +10.58      2.89 dex
           12-20   87.51%       95.96%   +8.45      2.33 dex

   The normalization advantage falls from +14.2 points on the full grid to
   +8.4 on the narrowest band, tracking the shrinking spread of absolute transit depth.
